1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,858
A bank surveys its customers and records two numbers for each:

2
00:00:03,958 --> 00:00:07,944
monthly income and monthly spending. The card team asks how much

3
00:00:08,044 --> 00:00:12,285
customers spend. The credit team asks how much customers earning six

4
00:00:12,385 --> 00:00:15,860
hundred manat spend. Same data, two different questions.

5
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:20,956
Both answers are already inside the joint table. One comes from adding

6
00:00:21,056 --> 00:00:23,772
it up, the other from cutting a slice out of it.

7
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:28,596
Here is the joint table. Each cell is the share of customers with

8
00:00:28,696 --> 00:00:32,368
that income and that spending, and all nine cells add up to one.

9
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:35,260
The card team does not care about income. So

10
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,482
squash each column down: add the three cells, and

11
00:00:38,582 --> 00:00:42,823
write the total underneath. Thirty-two percent spend five hundred,

12
00:00:42,923 --> 00:00:47,360
forty-one percent nine hundred, twenty-seven percent fifteen hundred.

13
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:52,105
Add across the rows instead, and you get the distribution of income alone.

14
00:00:52,205 --> 00:00:56,347
These totals are written in the margins of the table, which is why

15
00:00:56,447 --> 00:01:01,681
they are called marginal distributions. Summing out the other variable is all there

16
00:01:01,781 --> 00:01:05,087
is to it; for densities, the sum becomes an integral.

17
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:12,126
Now the credit team. They only care about customers earning six hundred.

18
00:01:12,226 --> 00:01:15,520
So throw away every other row, and keep this one slice.

19
00:01:15,616 --> 00:01:18,210
On their own these three numbers add up to only

20
00:01:18,310 --> 00:01:21,132
thirty percent, so they are not yet a distribution.

21
00:01:22,216 --> 00:01:25,301
Divide each one by the row total, zero point three. The

22
00:01:25,401 --> 00:01:28,543
bars keep their shape but stretch until they add to one.

23
00:01:28,643 --> 00:01:33,002
That is the conditional distribution: two thirds of six-hundred earners spend

24
00:01:33,102 --> 00:01:36,823
five hundred, and fewer than one in fifteen spend fifteen hundred.

25
00:01:36,916 --> 00:01:41,089
Cut the slice for the richest group instead, and the conditional

26
00:01:41,189 --> 00:01:45,628
distribution tips the other way: sixty percent of them spend fifteen

27
00:01:45,728 --> 00:01:50,034
hundred. Conditioning is simply choosing a slice and rescaling it.

28
00:01:51,133 --> 00:01:55,590
The same two moves work for densities. A filling station starts the day

29
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:59,763
with Y two thousand litres of diesel and sells Y one, which can't

30
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:03,807
exceed the stock. The joint density is flat over this triangle.

31
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:06,982
For the marginal of the stock, fix Y two and

32
00:02:07,082 --> 00:02:11,250
integrate across: the horizontal slice has length y two, so

33
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:15,157
the marginal density is y two over eight. Higher stock

34
00:02:15,257 --> 00:02:18,846
levels have longer slices, and so more probability.

35
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:23,284
And the conditional? Suppose we know the station opened with three

36
00:02:23,384 --> 00:02:27,196
thousand litres. Take that slice, and rescale it so it has

37
00:02:27,296 --> 00:02:30,906
area one. It is flat, so sales are uniform between zero

38
00:02:31,006 --> 00:02:34,480
and three: the joint density divided by the marginal.

39
00:02:36,066 --> 00:02:41,552
So: the joint distribution holds everything. A marginal adds the other variable

40
00:02:41,652 --> 00:02:46,148
out and forgets it. A conditional keeps one slice and rescales it

41
00:02:46,248 --> 00:02:49,471
to total one. No new data is needed for either.

